🚀 𝐇𝐨𝐰 𝐇𝐨𝐭𝐬𝐭𝐚𝐫 𝐇𝐚𝐧𝐝𝐥𝐞𝐝 25.3 𝐌𝐢𝐥𝐥𝐢𝐨𝐧 𝐂𝐨𝐧𝐜𝐮𝐫𝐫𝐞𝐧𝐭 𝐕𝐢𝐞𝐰𝐞𝐫𝐬 𝐈𝐧 𝐭𝐡𝐞 2019 𝐂𝐫𝐢𝐜𝐤𝐞𝐭 𝐖𝐨𝐫𝐥𝐝 𝐂𝐮𝐩: 𝐀 𝐃𝐞𝐯𝐎𝐩𝐬 & 𝐒𝐜𝐚𝐥𝐚𝐛𝐢𝐥𝐢𝐭𝐲 𝐒𝐮𝐜𝐜𝐞𝐬𝐬 𝐒𝐭𝐨𝐫𝐲! 🚀
In the 2019 Cricket World Cup, while India experienced a heartbreaking moment on the field, something extraordinary happened in the world of technology! Hotstar set a world record with 25.3 million concurrent viewers streaming the match live. 📺🔥
But have you ever wondered how Hotstar managed this massive traffic without crashing? Let’s break it down step by step! ⬇️

1️⃣ 𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗖𝗵𝗮𝗹𝗹𝗲𝗻𝗴𝗲: 𝗛𝗮𝗻𝗱𝗹𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗮 𝗧𝘀𝘂𝗻𝗮𝗺𝗶 𝗼𝗳 𝗩𝗶𝗲𝘄𝗲𝗿𝘀 🌊
Imagine a single server can handle only 1,000 users at a time. Now, think about millions of people watching a live stream together. Without a proper system in place, the entire platform would crash instantly!
Hotstar had to handle sudden spikes in traffic, especially during crucial moments in the match, such as MS Dhoni walking in to bat or getting run out – events that caused viewership to explode within seconds.
This extreme, unpredictable traffic pattern is known as the Tsunami Effect .

2️⃣ 𝗧𝗿𝗮𝗳𝗳𝗶𝗰 𝗣𝗮𝘁𝘁𝗲𝗿𝗻𝘀 & 𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗧𝘀𝘂𝗻𝗮𝗺𝗶 𝗘𝗳𝗳𝗲𝗰𝘁 📈
During live sports events, user traffic is highly unpredictable. Some key moments in the match caused traffic to skyrocket: 
✅ 2 million users joined instantly at the start.
✅ Dropped by 1 million within a few minutes.
✅ Jumped from 1M to 10M as the match got intense.
✅ Peaked at 25.3 million when MS Dhoni came to bat.
✅ Dropped to near zero within seconds after he got out.

Handling such drastic fluctuations required a well-designed, scalable, and automated infrastructure.

3️⃣ 𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗛𝗼𝘁𝘀𝘁𝗮𝗿 𝗦𝗰𝗮𝗹𝗲𝗱 𝘁𝗼 𝗛𝗮𝗻𝗱𝗹𝗲 𝟮𝟱.𝟯 𝗠𝗶𝗹𝗹𝗶𝗼𝗻 𝗨𝘀𝗲𝗿𝘀? ⚙️
To manage this massive surge in traffic, Hotstar used: 
✅ Dynamic Scaling – When one server reached its limit, another server was automatically launched.
✅ Traffic Prediction (MLOps) – Machine learning algorithms analyzed past user data to predict traffic spikes.
✅ Automation & DevOps – Manual intervention was impossible, so automated deployment & load balancing ensured seamless performance. 
✅ Resilient Infrastructure – If the system failed, millions of users would have been lost, leading to huge business losses.

5️⃣ 𝗥𝗼𝗹𝗲 𝗼𝗳 𝗔𝘂𝘁𝗼𝗺𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻, 𝗗𝗲𝘃𝗢𝗽𝘀 & 𝗠𝗟𝗢𝗽𝘀 🤖
🔹 DevOps & Site Reliability Engineering (SRE): Ensured real-time monitoring and quick response to traffic surges.
 🔹 MLOps & AI: Predicted traffic patterns, allowing resources to be allocated efficiently.
 🔹 Automated CI/CD Pipelines: Allowed smooth updates and scaling without disrupting the live stream.
These practices ensured zero downtime, reduced costs, and a seamless viewing experience.
